{"name":"Baby Blue","hex":"#a2cffe","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Baby Blue","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a number of autonomous subsidiaries of IBM Corporation, formed from 1991 to make the company more manageable and to streamline investment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Baby_Blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Baby blue","blocks":[{"text":"Baby blue, also called light blue, is a tint of azure, which is one of the pastel colors."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baby_blue"},{"title":"Baby Blue (Badfinger song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Baby Blue\" is a song by Welsh rock band Badfinger, from their fourth studio album, Straight Up (1971). The song was written by Pete Ham, produced by Todd Rundgren, and released on Apple Records. As a single in the US in 1972, it went to No. 14."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/22/Baby_blue_sleeve.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baby_Blue_(Badfinger_song)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"htt